      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "By the end of the 1860s, the Alabama‑Florida Railroad had built a depot in Fort Deposit, linking Montgomery to Florida.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/fort-deposit/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1935, a gas station in Fort Deposit became the birthplace of Priester’s Pecans, now a global pecan brand.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/fort-deposit/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During the civil‑rights era, about 30 activists were arrested in Fort Deposit and sent to a larger jail in Hayneville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/fort-deposit/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Every year, Fort Deposit hosts the Calico Fort arts and crafts fair on the second weekend of April.",
